$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM 2-Ethylthiophene KOH (0.57 g, 10 mmol) was added to triethylene glycol
(5.0 mL) and heated to 100 °C until it dissolved to give a red/orange
solution. Methyl (E)-2-(1-(thiophen-2-yl)ethylidene)hydrazine-1-
carboxylate (0.50 g, 2.5 mmol) was added in one portion and the reaction
heated at 140 °C for 4 hours. The reaction was allowed to cool, diluted
with water and the aqueous phase extracted into Et2O (2 × 10 mL). The
organic phase was dried (MgSO4), filtered and the solvent removed in
vacuo. The crude product was purified by flash column chromatography (10%
Et2O/pentane). 2-ethylthiophene as a pale yellow oil (0.22 g, 2.0 mmol,
77%). Product contains a trace of the corresponding azine (<5%). Only
major peaks reported. 1H NMR (400 MHz, CDCl3) δ 7.06 (1H, dd, J = 5.1,
1.2 Hz, CHAr), 6.91 (1H, dd, J = 5.1, 3.4 Hz, CHAr), 6.78 (1H, dd, J =
3.4, 1.1 Hz, CHAr), 3.91 (2H, q, J = 7.5 Hz, CH2CH3), 1.31 (3H, t, J =
7.5 Hz, CH2CH3). 13C NMR (100 MHz, CDCl3) δ 147.5 (CAr), 126.7 (CArH),
123.3 (CArH), 122.7 (CArH), 23.3 (CH2CH3), 16.1 (CH2CH3). νmax (neat)
cm-1 3073, 2966, 2926, 2852, 1591, 1429, 1363, 1290.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):NOTES
$DATUM Wolff-Kishner reduction, triethylene glycol also acts as solvent in stage
1
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):TITLE
$DATUM Methyl Hydrazinocarboxylate as a Practical Alternative to Hydrazine in
the Wolff-Kishner Reaction
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):AUTHOR
$DATUM Cranwell, Philippa B.; Russell, Andrew T.; Smith, Christopher D.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):CITATION
$DATUM Synlett (2016), 27(1), 131-135

