$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM Ethyl benzene KOH (14 g, 250 mmol) was added to triethylene glycol (40
mL) and heated to 100 °C until it dissolved to give a red/orange
solution. Methyl (E)-2-(1-phenylethylidene)hydrazine-1-carboxylate (8.0
g, 42 mmol) was added in one portion and the reaction heated at 140 °C
for 4 hours. The reaction was allowed to cool, diluted with water and the
aqueous phase extracted into Et2O (2 × 50 mL). The organic phase was
dried (MgSO4), filtered and the solvent removed in vacuo. The crude
product was purified by flash column chromatography (100% pentane). Ethyl
benzene as a yellow oil (3.1 g, 29 mmol, 70%). 1H NMR (400 MHz, CDCl3): δ
7.29-7.17 (5H, m, CHAr), 2.64 (2H, q, J = 7.6 Hz, CH2CH3), 1.24 (3H, t, J
= 7.6 Hz, CH2CH3). 13C NMR (100 MHz, CDCl3): δ 144.3 (CAr), 128.3 (CArH),
127.9 (CArH), 125.6 (CArH), 28.9 (CH2CH3), 15.6 (CH2CH3).
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):NOTES
$DATUM Wolff-Kishner reduction, triethylene glycol also acts as solvent in stage
1
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):TITLE
$DATUM Methyl Hydrazinocarboxylate as a Practical Alternative to Hydrazine in
the Wolff-Kishner Reaction
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):AUTHOR
$DATUM Cranwell, Philippa B.; Russell, Andrew T.; Smith, Christopher D.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):CITATION
$DATUM Synlett (2016), 27(1), 131-135

